# Both spellings on purpose. The trailing slash matches a directory only, so a
# node_modules *symlink* — what you get pointing a scratch worktree at another
# checkout's install — slips past it and lands in a commit, where it breaks CI
# with ENOTDIR on the next install.
node_modules/
node_modules
dist/
build/
.next/
.next-build/
# Generated by Next on every `next dev` and `next build`, naming whichever
# distDir was active — and next.config.mjs deliberately gives those two
# different ones so a build cannot replace the chunks a dev server is serving.
# Tracked, that made the file flip between `.next` and `.next-build` depending
# on which command ran last, so anyone who built and then ran the dev server
# had a dirty tree they did not cause. Nothing needs it committed: it is
# recreated by dev and by build, and `pnpm typecheck` passes without it (the
# tsconfig `include` entry is a glob, and a missing file drops out of it).
apps/web/next-env.d.ts
out/
coverage/
bin/
*.tsbuildinfo

.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

.DS_Store
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# local agent workspaces (hosted-runner clones + local-mode scratch)
.specd-work/

# isolated build worktrees
.specd-build/
apps/api/vitest-report.json

# The published site is generated from apps/web/lib/docs by `pnpm site:build`
# and uploaded straight to Pages by CI. Committing the output would mean two
# sources of truth for the same words, and the stale one always wins an
# argument nobody knew they were having.
#
# Leading slash on purpose: a bare `site/` matches at any depth, which also
# swallowed scripts/site/ — the generator's own source — and hid it from
# `git status` entirely.
/site/
